feat(vol): replace the userdata HFS+ volume with a host-made image

The kernel mounts an HFS+ root on userdata, but that volume is only 64 MiB:
the 592 MB .symbols install stops at 64,028,672 bytes with ENOSPC, and the
3.3 GB split dyld shared cache cannot start.  The volume header's totalBlocks
sets the size, so the partition being 229 GiB does not help; the volume has to
be rebuilt.

Nothing in the kernel formats HFS+.  This adds the receiving end of a volume
imprint that Apple's own newfs_hfs prepares on the host:

  - scripts/make-root-volume.py creates a sparse raw image, runs newfs_hfs -v
    KebabRoot -b 4096 on it, reads the new volume header and its allocation
    bitmap, and writes a VEXT v1 stream: a 32-byte header (magic, version,
    volume bytes, block size, extent count, data bytes), then records of
    {u64 offset, u32 length, u32 reserved, data}, then zero padding to a
    64 KiB boundary.  Only blocks the new volume allocates are shipped; the
    measured 16 GiB volume needs 426 of 4,194,304 blocks, 3 extents and
    1,744,896 bytes of extent data.
  - bsd/dev/kebab_vol.c parses that stream forward, validates every field and
    every extent against the declared volume and the userdata partition,
    captures the primary volume header from the stream and checks it before
    the first block is written, then writes each extent through the UFS block
    path.  After the last extent it reads the header back from the device,
    compares all 512 bytes with the stream, and prints both headers.  Every
    failure is a printed VOL ABORT/FAIL; nothing here panics.
  - bsd/dev/kebab_ufs.c grows a raw block path (kebab_ufs_raw_write/read)
    that bypasses the buffer cache and re-checks offset and length against the
    userdata partition under the UFS lock.  There is no other device to write.
  - bsd/dev/kebab_usb.c carries VOL BEGIN/DRY?/VOL? over the existing bulk OUT
    transport, one 64 KiB chunk per command and one "VOL ACK <cumulative>"
    line back, reusing the installer's buffer and pending/ACK discipline.  A
    chunk is routed by the stream that armed it, not by the current mode.
  - the command family is behind boot argument kebab_vol=1 (default off), and
    a real write additionally needs the literal CONFIRM and a volume size that
    fits inside userdata.  kebab_vol=1 also keeps the old root read-only even
    when rootrw=1: the mounted HFS+ must not flush a cached block over the new
    metadata.  The next, ordinary boot mounts the new volume read-write.
  - the HFS+ mount log now prints the mounted volume's block size, total
    blocks and free blocks, so the 64 MiB -> 16 GiB change is visible without
    another tool.

The old volume's header is printed as fields and as all 512 raw bytes before
anything is written; the volume's contents are destroyed by design and are
restored from the host with the existing KIST installer.

The host side is measured: 16 GiB, 4096-byte blocks, 4,194,304 blocks, 426
allocated, 3 extents, 1,744,896 bytes of extent data and a 1,769,472-byte
stream.  The same kebab_vol.c compiled for the macOS host reconstructs a volume
whose 426 allocated blocks and both volume headers are byte-identical to the
newfs_hfs image and which fsck_hfs reports OK; a corrupted header and an
oversized volume both abort with zero write calls.  The on-board imprint, the
mount log showing 4,194,304 blocks, INST? free space and the 592,101,376-byte
install are still pending -- the board was stranded in a panic when this was
written -- and docs/BRING-UP-STATE.md section 63 carries the exact commands and
expected output for that run.
